{% extends "layout/gates.twig" %}

{# ── THE TICKET ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   Reachable with the reference alone, which is the same doctrine as the claim link, the
   interview page and the nominee questionnaire: an attendee has no account, and putting a
   login between somebody and the door they are standing at is how a queue stops moving.

   ── WHAT THIS IS BUILT AGAINST ──────────────────────────────────────────────

   Two design documents, and they specify two DIFFERENT artefacts from the same markup:

     • `Mobile Ticket — Spec.dc.html` — the stub a guest opens on a phone. 390 wide, 1087
       tall, dark frame, cream stub. Its numbers are reproduced exactly below and they are
       marked · SPEC · where they are, so nobody "tidies" one of them later.
     • `Event Ticket.dc.html` (artboard 1a) — the printed stub. 148 × 62 mm landscape, art
       left, tear, stub right. NOT a screenshot of the screen layout; the spec is explicit
       that print is specified separately, and the print block at the bottom of this file
       rebuilds the same DOM into that shape.

   ── THE ANATOMY, AND WHY IT IS THIS ONE ─────────────────────────────────────

   A ticket is one of the few documents where convention IS the usability, so the shape is
   the one people already know how to read:

     • A HERO CARRYING THE EVENT'S OWN IMAGE, with the title over a wash of the organiser's
       accent so the text stays legible whatever the photograph is doing underneath.
     • A PERFORATION — a cream strip, two notches punched in the frame colour, one dashed
       rule. It says "tear here" without a word of explanation.
     • A STUB holding the QR and the code. That is where a hand goes and where a scanner is
       pointed, and it clears an 844px fold so it is never the thing below the screen.
     • A DETAIL GRID rather than a list of rows, so the holder, the tier and the address are
       findable at arm's length instead of read in order.
     • AND THE ACTIONS OUTSIDE THE STUB, so the ticket itself is a clean screenshot and the
       print block can drop them without leaving a hole in the document.

   ── WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, BECAUSE IT MUST NOT ────────────────────────────────

     • THE CODE IS STILL THE POINT. Large, monospaced, widely tracked, high contrast.
     • QR AND PRINTED CODE, ALWAYS BOTH — never one or the other. A camera fails; a printed
       code does not. They carry the same string so they cannot disagree.
     • NO SCRIPT IS NEEDED to show any of it. A ticket that needs JavaScript to render is a
       ticket that sometimes does not.
     • IT PRINTS, and prints without the site's navigation across it.
     • IT SAYS WHAT IS STILL OWED. A pending payment shows NO CODE AT ALL, because a pending
       payment rendered as a scannable ticket is an argument at a door.

   ── AND WHY THE COLOURS COME FROM PHP ───────────────────────────────────────

   `design` is resolved by {@see \AfricaGates\Services\EventTicketDesign}, which refuses
   anything that is not a hex colour rather than escaping it. The accent lands inside a
   `style` attribute, which makes it the one value on the organiser's form that is executed
   rather than displayed — so the template is handed a colour it can trust and does no
   validation of its own. `ink` and `accent_soft` are derived there too: an organiser who
   picks a pale accent gets dark text handed back automatically, because the alternative is
   a ticket nobody can read and no explanation of why.

   The organiser's THEME now chooses the frame the ticket sits in rather than the ticket
   itself. `dark` is the spec's #10292C ground and the default; `light` is the same document
   on the platform's paper ground, for an organiser whose event is not a night event. The
   hero is the accent and the stub is cream in both, because those two are what make the
   thing legible and scannable and neither is a matter of taste.
#}

{% set D = design %}
{% set dark = D.theme == 'dark' %}

{% block head_styles %}
<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TOKENS

     Every value further down resolves to one of these. Nothing below hard-codes a
     hex except inside `@media print`, where the palette is deliberately forced.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  /* ── the page the ticket sits on ────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · #f0f2f2, which is the design document's own ground, and the dark frame
     centred on it at 390. At phone width the frame goes full bleed, so the page has
     to match it or a band of white opens up under the ticket and reads as a bug.

     Scoped to `body[data-page="events"]`, which is only ever emitted from a page's
     own `head_styles` — so this rule ships on the ticket page and nowhere else. */
  body[data-page="events"]{ background:#f0f2f2; }
  @media (max-width:430px){
    body[data-page="events"]{ background:{{ dark ? '#10292C' : '#f0f2f2' }}; }
  }
  @media print{ body[data-page="events"]{ background:#fff; } }

  .tk{
    /* the event's own accent, resolved and validated in PHP */
    --tk-accent:{{ D.accent }}; --tk-ink:{{ D.ink }}; --tk-soft:{{ D.accent_soft }};

    /* ── INK OVER THE HERO ────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The kicker is gold in the design document, and the design document assumes a
       dark accent. An organiser may pick a pale one — the form allows any hex — and
       gold on pale gold is a date nobody can read, which defeats the reason the date
       is on a ticket at all.

       So these three follow `D.ink`, which EventTicketDesign computes from the
       accent's own luminance and returns as exactly `#FFFFFF` or `#10292C`. On a
       light hero the gold steps down to --gold-700, which is the same rule the spec
       states for gold on cream. */
    --tk-hero-gold:{{ D.ink == '#FFFFFF' ? '#f3b416' : '#745001' }};
    --tk-hero-title:{{ D.ink == '#FFFFFF' ? '#fbf6e6' : '#10292C' }};
    --tk-hero-soft:{{ D.ink == '#FFFFFF' ? 'rgba(251,246,230,.78)' : 'rgba(16,41,44,.72)' }};

    /* · SPEC · the ticket's own two grounds. Not organiser-settable: the stub is
       cream because a QR needs a light quiet zone, and the cream is what makes the
       dashed tear read as a tear rather than as a border. */
    --tk-stub:#f6f2e6;
    --tk-cream:#fbf6e6;
    --tk-gold:#f3b416;

    /* · SPEC · #626a6e is the floor for small type on light ground — never lighter.
       #10292C is every dark word on the stub. */
    --tk-text:#10292C;
    --tk-mute:#626a6e;
    --tk-line:rgba(16,41,44,.12);

    /* The frame. Light theme: the platform's own paper. Gold steps down to
       --gold-700 on it, because #f3b416 on a light ground misses 4.5:1 and the
       kicker is 9.5px type. */
    --tk-frame:#f0f2f2;
    --tk-on-frame:#4a5256;
    --tk-on-frame-2:#626a6e;
    --tk-frame-gold:#745001;
    --tk-frame-line:rgba(16,41,44,.24);

    /* · SPEC · 390 wide, border-box, 52 / 20 / 30 padding. Content runs past 844,
       so the page scrolls — the hero, the QR and the code all clear the fold and
       adding an optional row pushes the ACTIONS down, never the scan block. */
    box-sizing:border-box; width:100%; max-width:390px; margin:24px auto 40px;
    padding:52px 20px 30px; border-radius:34px;
    background:var(--tk-frame); color:var(--tk-on-frame);
    display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:20px;
  }
  .tk--dark{
    --tk-frame:#10292C;
    /* .62 rather than the .45 in the artboard. At .45 this lands at 3.9:1 against
       #10292C and the spec's own floor for anything under 18px is 4.5:1 — the
       reference contradicts its own rule, so the rule wins. .62 measures 6.1:1. */
    --tk-on-frame:rgba(251,246,230,.86);
    --tk-on-frame-2:rgba(251,246,230,.62);
    --tk-frame-gold:var(--tk-gold);
    /* .38, not .22: a control's own boundary is non-text UI and wants 3:1. The
       artboard value measures 1.9:1, which is a button nobody can see the edge of. */
    --tk-frame-line:rgba(251,246,230,.38);
  }
  /* Full bleed at phone width. The frame IS the phone there, so its corners are the
     device's and drawing a second set inside them is a card, not a ticket. The 20px
     side padding is kept, which is what makes the inner column 350px at 390. */
  @media (max-width:430px){
    .tk{ max-width:none; margin:0; border-radius:0; }
  }

  /* ── the header row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · mono 9.5px, ADMIT ONE at .24em in gold, the reference at .16em. This
     is the only place the reference appears on screen: it is what somebody reads
     out to support, and the floating help button covers the bottom-right of the
     viewport, so it goes at the top. */
  .tk__meta{ display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:12px; }
  .tk__admit{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:9.5px; font-weight:700;
    letter-spacing:.24em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--tk-frame-gold); }
  .tk__ref{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:9.5px; letter-spacing:.16em;
    color:var(--tk-on-frame-2); text-align:right; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }

  /* ── the document ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One elevation for the whole artefact rather than a shadow per panel, so the
     hero, the tear and the stub read as one piece of card. */
  .tk__doc{ position:relative; display:flex; flex-direction:column;
    box-shadow:0 24px 56px -30px rgba(0,0,0,.7); }
  .tk:not(.tk--dark) .tk__doc{ box-shadow:0 24px 56px -34px rgba(16,41,44,.5); }

  /* ── the hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · a 268px band, 20px radius on the top two corners only. */
  .tk__hero{ position:relative; height:268px; overflow:hidden; border-radius:20px 20px 0 0;
    background:var(--tk-accent); color:var(--tk-ink); }
  /* `object-fit:cover`, and a fallback focal point for the images nobody has framed.

     The artboard pins `left:-150 top:-178 width:700 height:875` against the band,
     which frames the subject of one particular poster and means nothing for the
     next event's. The spec named the real fix — a dedicated 3:2 crop stored per
     event rather than a poster cropped in CSS — and that now exists: the admin's
     artwork editor writes one with AfricaGates\Services\TicketArtwork, and
     `D.image` is the 1200×800 render of it.

     These two lines are what happens for everyone else: an event still using its
     cover photograph, or an organiser who pasted an address the editor cannot cut.
     `50% 32%` keeps faces and figures in frame on portrait artwork, which is what
     event covers overwhelmingly are; on a framed image it is a no-op, because a 3:2
     render into a 390×268 band has about 3% of width to spare and nothing to
     choose between. The wash below is what keeps the type legible either way. */
  .tk__shot{ position:absolute; inset:0; width:100%; height:100%;
    object-fit:cover; object-position:50% 32%; }
  /* The wash: the accent, bottom to top. It is what makes the title legible over an
     unknown photograph, and it is what keeps EventTicketDesign::contrastInk's promise
     true — that computes readable ink for the ACCENT, and the promise only holds if
     the accent is what is actually behind the words.

     ── WHERE THIS DEVIATES FROM THE DESIGN DOCUMENT, AND WHY ────────────────
     The artboard's stops are 97% at the foot, 90% at 22%, 42% at 42% and clear by
     66%, measured against one dark purple poster. The type block ends 36% up the band
     on a one-line title and 46% up on a wrapped one, so on that curve the 9.5px date
     lands on the accent at roughly 66% alpha. Over a LIGHT cover that measures
     3.1:1 — under the 4.5:1 floor the same spec sets for anything below 18px, and the
     date is the single most useful line on a ticket.

     This repository has already been here: the previous ticket ran this wash at D9,
     "a light illustration showed through and the date became unreadable", and that is
     why it shipped nearly opaque. So the curve keeps the artboard's SHAPE — artwork at
     the top, accent at the foot — with the alpha held above 95% through the whole type
     block and clear by 78%. The top 38% of the band is still the artwork. */
  .tk__scrim{ position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .tk__title{ position:absolute; left:22px; right:22px; bottom:20px;
    display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px; }
  .tk__kick{ margin:0; font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:9.5px;
    font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.26em; text-transform:uppercase;
    color:var(--tk-hero-gold); }
  .tk__title h1{ margin:0; font-family:var(--ag-font-display, Georgia, serif);
    font-size:40px; font-weight:900; line-height:1; letter-spacing:-.025em;
    color:var(--tk-hero-title); text-wrap:balance; overflow-wrap:break-word; }
  /* Two lines is the ceiling at 390. A longer title steps down rather than eating
     the image — this is the one string on the page with no length limit upstream. */
  .tk__title h1.is-long{ font-size:30px; line-height:1.06; letter-spacing:-.02em; }
  .tk__sub{ margin:0; font-size:11px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:.16em;
    text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--tk-hero-soft); }

  /* ── the perforation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · a 20px cream strip, two 22px circles in the FRAME colour pushed half
     outside each edge, and a 1px dashed rule at 28% ink. The notches take the frame
     colour rather than white so they still read as holes on either theme. */
  .tk__tear{ position:relative; height:20px; background:var(--tk-stub); }
  .tk__tear::before, .tk__tear::after{ content:""; position:absolute; top:50%;
    width:22px; height:22px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--tk-frame);
    transform:translateY(-50%); }
  .tk__tear::before{ left:-11px }
  .tk__tear::after{ right:-11px }
  .tk__tear i{ position:absolute; left:16px; right:16px; top:50%; height:0;
    border-top:1px dashed rgba(16,41,44,.28); }

  /* ── the stub ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · cream, 20px radius on the bottom two corners, 10 / 24 / 26 padding,
     22px between blocks.

     TWO ELEMENTS, not one, and they abut so that on screen they are one cream panel.
     The split exists for the printed sheet: there the scan block becomes the tear-off
     stub down the right-hand side and the facts become a band under the artwork, and
     a grid can only place children it actually has. Doing it with `display:contents`
     on a single panel loses the panel's own background and its dashed edge, which are
     the two things the printed stub is made of. */
  .tk__stub{ background:var(--tk-stub); padding:10px 24px 0; color:var(--tk-text);
    display:flex; flex-direction:column; }
  .tk__facts{ background:var(--tk-stub); border-radius:0 0 20px 20px;
    padding:0 24px 26px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:22px;
    color:var(--tk-text); }

  /* the scan block · SPEC · 140px symbol, 17px mono code at .22em */
  .tk__scan{ display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; gap:12px; }
  /* The white ground is Qr::svg's own quiet zone, and it is kept rather than
     recoloured to the cream: the quiet zone is what a decoder uses to find the
     symbol's edge, and "pure black on white with no tint behind it" is the same
     rule the print block obeys. One code path for screen and paper means the thing
     that scans at a door is the thing that was tested. */
  .tk__qr{ padding:6px; line-height:0; }
  .tk__qr svg{ display:block; width:140px; height:140px; border-radius:3px; }
  .tk__codewrap{ display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; gap:3px; }
  .tk__code{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:17px; font-weight:700;
    letter-spacing:.22em; color:var(--tk-text); text-align:center; overflow-wrap:anywhere;
    /* The tracking is applied to the right of every glyph including the last, which
       walks the string off centre. Pulled back by one step. */
    margin-right:-.22em; }
  .tk__qrnote{ font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.01em; color:var(--tk-mute); }
  /* Print only — see the note beside it in the markup. */
  .tk__who{ display:none }

  /* the detail grid · SPEC · two columns, 18 / 16 gutters, over a hairline
     TWO columns and not one at every width. Collapsing doubles the height of
     everything under the fold for no gain — these are short labelled values, not
     paragraphs — and it is why the reference used to end up two screens down. */
  .tk__grid{ display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px 16px;
    padding-top:22px; border-top:1px solid var(--tk-line); }
  .tk__cell{ display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:3px; min-width:0; }
  .tk__cell small{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:8px; font-weight:700;
    letter-spacing:.22em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--tk-mute); }
  .tk__cell b{ font-size:13.5px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:-.005em; line-height:1.45;
    color:var(--tk-text); overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
  .tk__cell--wide{ grid-column:1 / -1 }
  /* Print only. The 46mm stub cannot carry the day name AND the time as two cells, so
     paper gets a joined line and the reference instead — and neither may appear on
     screen, where the same facts are already in the cells above and in the header. */
  .tk__cell--print{ display:none }
  /* The tier dot. A dot beside the name, never the name in colour: the tier is
     already named in words, so the colour is a second and faster way to recognise
     it and never the only way. The 1px edge is the palette's own `edge` value,
     which clears 3:1 by construction — without it a pale fill on cream is an
     absence rather than a mark. */
  .tk__dot{ display:inline-block; width:9px; height:9px; border-radius:50%;
    border:1px solid; margin-right:7px; vertical-align:baseline; flex:none; }

  .tk__note2{ font-size:12px; line-height:1.55; color:var(--tk-mute); }
  .tk__note2 b{ color:var(--tk-text); font-weight:700; }

  /* the signature row · SPEC · the mark on the left, the domain on the right */
  .tk__sig{ display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:12px;
    padding-top:18px; border-top:1px solid var(--tk-line); }
  .tk__sig img{ width:26px; height:26px; display:block; opacity:.9; }
  .tk__sig span{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace); font-size:9.5px;
    letter-spacing:.18em; color:var(--tk-mute); }
  /* Paper cannot be clicked. This is how somebody who printed a ticket and then
     lost the email finds it again, so on paper it is spelled out as text. */
  .tk__url{ display:none }

  /* ── the states ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Between the stub and the actions, per the spec. Tinted panels on the frame
     rather than white chips: a white chip on the dark ground reads as a second
     document, and the point of these is that they belong to the ticket. */
  .tk__flag{ border-radius:14px; padding:14px 16px; font-size:13px; line-height:1.65;
    border:1px solid; }
  .tk__flag strong{ font-weight:700 }
  .tk__flag--wait{ background:rgba(243,180,22,.12); border-color:rgba(243,180,22,.42);
    color:#6b5312; }
  .tk__flag--dead{ background:rgba(180,42,26,.09); border-color:rgba(180,42,26,.34);
    color:#7d2b24; }
  .tk__flag--in{ background:rgba(35,123,34,.10); border-color:rgba(35,123,34,.34);
    color:#1c5f1b; }
  .tk--dark .tk__flag--wait{ color:#ffd97a }
  .tk--dark .tk__flag--dead{ background:rgba(255,120,105,.12);
    border-color:rgba(255,120,105,.42); color:#ffb3a8; }
  .tk--dark .tk__flag--in{ background:rgba(143,213,139,.12);
    border-color:rgba(143,213,139,.4); color:#a8e0a4; }

  /* ── the actions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     · SPEC · outside the document, 46px tall, 12px radius, gold primary. Outside so
     the print block drops them without leaving a gap in the ticket, and so the
     ticket is a clean screenshot. */
  .tk__acts{ display:flex; gap:10px; margin-top:2px; }
  .tk__act{ flex:1; display:inline-flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; gap:7px;
    min-height:46px; padding:0 10px; border-radius:12px; font-size:13.5px; font-weight:700;
    letter-spacing:.01em; text-align:center; text-decoration:none; }
  .tk__act--go{ background:var(--tk-gold); color:#10292C; }
  .tk__act--go:hover{ background:#ffc63d }
  .tk__act--ghost{ border:1px solid var(--tk-frame-line); font-weight:500;
    color:var(--tk-on-frame); }
  .tk__act--ghost:hover{ border-color:var(--tk-frame-gold); color:var(--tk-frame-gold) }
  .tk__act svg{ width:15px; height:15px; flex:none }
  .tk__act:focus-visible{ outline:3px solid var(--tk-gold); outline-offset:3px }

  .tk__foot{ margin:2px 0 0; font-size:11px; line-height:1.7; color:var(--tk-on-frame-2);
    text-wrap:pretty; }
  .tk__foot + .tk__foot{ margin-top:10px }
  .tk__foot b{ color:var(--tk-on-frame) }
  .tk__foot a{ color:var(--tk-frame-gold); text-decoration:underline }

  /* ── the dead link ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A mistyped reference is a person with a ticket they cannot find, not an error. */
  .tk__lost{ background:var(--tk-stub); border-radius:20px; padding:26px 24px;
    color:var(--tk-text); }
  .tk__lost h1{ margin:0 0 10px; font-family:var(--ag-font-display, Georgia, serif);
    font-size:22px; font-weight:800; line-height:1.2; letter-spacing:-.02em; }
  .tk__lost p{ margin:0 0 10px; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.65; color:var(--tk-mute) }
  .tk__lost a{ color:#1a6118; font-weight:700; text-decoration:underline }

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     PRINT — a different artefact, not a screenshot

     A ticket gets printed far more often than the web pretends: a cracked phone, a
     battery that will not last the day, a door team working from paper, a sponsor's
     table of twelve. So this is designed, and the spec is explicit that it is
     specified separately rather than inherited from the screen.

     THE SHEET is 148 × 62 mm landscape — a cut-to-size stub. THE SPLIT is art left,
     stub right at 46mm, with the fold a real perforation rather than the drawn
     notches used on screen. Every rule below exists because the paper fails without
     it:

       · THE QR IS SIZED IN MILLIMETRES. It is the one thing on the page whose size
         is a physical fact rather than a typographic taste. 26mm is the spec's
         floor; below about 22mm a handheld scanner starts failing on inkjet output.
         Left in px it prints at whatever the browser felt like, which is how a
         ticket comes off the printer unscannable and nobody finds out until the
         door.
       · print-color-adjust:exact, or the driver strips the accent ground and the
         ticket prints as a white rectangle.
       · THE QR NEVER SITS ON A TINT. The stub stays cream even if the accent is
         dark: the code needs the contrast more than the design needs the colour.
       · INK-SAFE TYPE. 6pt floor for labels, 8.5pt for values, 9pt mono for the
         code. The screen scale is too fine once it is halftoned.
       · AND THE URL IS PRINTED AS TEXT, because paper cannot be clicked and the
         link is how a lost ticket is recovered.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  /* ── THE SHEET IS NOT THE TICKET ──────────────────────────────────────────
     This used to read `size:148mm 62mm landscape`, which is not valid CSS: `size`
     takes either two lengths OR a named page size with an orientation keyword, never
     both. The whole declaration was therefore dropped — margin included — and every
     ticket printed onto a default Letter page, jammed into the corner with no margin
     and nothing to say where to cut. Measured, not guessed: with that declaration
     Chromium reports a 215.9 × 279.4mm MediaBox, and with the keyword removed it
     reports 148.2 × 62.1mm.

     Removing the keyword would have made it work. It is not what this does, because a
     card-sized @page is the wrong thing to ask for: no home printer has 148 × 62mm
     media, so the driver scales the page to whatever is loaded — the ticket comes out
     28% oversized on A4 and cannot be cut to the size it was designed at.

     So the sheet is left alone. No `size`, which means the paper the person actually
     chose; a real margin; and the ticket laid on it at its true 148 × 62mm with a cut
     line around it. A4 and Letter both have well over 148mm of printable width, so the
     same rule is right on both without asking which one it is. */
  @page{ margin:10mm }

  @media print{
    /* Force the light palette onto BOTH themes. Written on `.tk` rather than
       `:root` so it cannot leak into anything else the page happens to print. */
    .tk, .tk--dark{ --tk-frame:#fff; --tk-line:#b9c4c3; --tk-text:#000; --tk-mute:#3d4749;
      --tk-stub:#f6f2e6; --tk-on-frame:#000; --tk-on-frame-2:#3d4749; }

    /* Centred at its true size on whatever paper turned up. `margin:0 auto` and not
       `margin:0`: against the left edge of an A4 it reads as a printing error rather
       than as a ticket somebody is meant to cut out. */
    .tk{ width:180mm; height:86mm; max-width:none; min-height:0; margin:0 auto; padding:0;
      border-radius:0; background:#fff; display:block; gap:0;
      /* An OUTLINE, not a border: outlines take no space, so the cut guide cannot push
         the ticket off its own measurements. 2mm out, which is the width of a pair of
         scissors' error and leaves the line itself on the offcut. */
      outline:.25mm dashed #9aa8a6; outline-offset:2mm;
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact; }
    /* Said once, under the ticket, in the smallest type on the sheet. A printed page
       with a card floating in the middle of it and no instruction is a page people
       fold instead of cutting. */
    .tk__cut{ display:block; width:180mm; margin:7mm auto 0; font-size:7.5pt;
      line-height:1.4; color:#5a6462; text-align:center }
    /* ── THE SHEET, AND WHY THE DETAILS PRINT UNDER THE ART ─────────────────
       Art left, tear, stub right at 46mm — the artboard's split, and the spec's.

       What the artboard cannot do is carry the whole field set in that 46mm column.
       Measured in a real print render: the sheet gives 215px of usable stub height at
       96dpi, a 26mm symbol takes 98 of it, and at the spec's own ink-safe scale — 6pt
       labels, 8.5pt values — four labelled rows plus the recovery URL need 145 more.
       That is 43px of overflow, and the thing that falls off the bottom of a
       cut-to-size stub is whatever comes last.

       The QR size is not negotiable (below ~22mm a handheld reader starts failing on
       printed output) and neither is the type floor (6pt is what survives halftoning).
       So the LAYOUT gives way: the left column becomes two rows — the artwork, then a
       cream band carrying the details in three columns, where 102mm of width means
       nothing wraps. The stub keeps the code, the name and the URL, which is what a
       tear-off half is for.

       Every fact still lands on a light ground, which is the point of the whole
       arrangement: `print-color-adjust` is a request a driver may refuse, and a ticket
       whose date and address were only ever on the accent fill prints as a blank. */
    /* ── 190 × 86MM, THE SAME CARD THE PDF PRINTS ───────────────────────────
       This was 148 × 62 — the artboard's own measurements — and the PDF path has always
       been 190 × 86, because a 30mm QR is the size below which the scanners a venue
       actually owns start hunting for the symbol, and a stub that holds one cannot be
       46mm wide. Two print paths producing two differently-sized tickets is a defect on
       its own: the same booking came out as two different objects depending on which
       button somebody pressed.
       
       So the browser follows the PDF, and 148 × 62 remains what it always was — the
       shape the design was drawn at, now scaled to a card that fits a real scanner. It
       still sits well inside A4's 190mm of printable width at a 10mm margin, and inside
       Letter's 196mm. Everything below is re-measured against the new card rather than
       multiplied: the artwork gains proportionally more than the type does, because at
       62mm the picture was down to a 4.4:1 letterbox. */
    .tk__doc{ width:180mm; height:86mm; box-shadow:none; overflow:hidden;
      display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 56mm; grid-template-rows:1fr auto;
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact; }
    .tk__hero{ grid-area:1 / 1 }
    .tk__facts{ grid-area:2 / 1 }
    .tk__stub{ grid-area:1 / 2 / span 2 / span 1 }

    /* `display:none`, not the `all:unset` in the spec's snippet. The tear is the
       document's third child, and unset leaves it an in-flow grid item — so it
       takes the 46mm column and pushes the stub onto an implicit second row, which
       is off the sheet. The fold is physical here; nothing is drawn for it. */
    .tk__tear{ display:none }

    /* ── ART LEFT, AND THE IMAGE COMES OUT OF THE POSITIONED LAYER ──────────
       On screen `.tk__shot` is `position:absolute; inset:0` and the title floats over
       its foot on a gradient. That is the right thing for a screen and the wrong thing
       for paper twice over.

       It is fragile: an absolutely-positioned element inside a paginated box is the
       single most inconsistently-handled thing in print CSS, and this is the one part
       of the document that is a photograph — when an engine drops it, the ticket prints
       with a blank band and nothing says why.

       And it is wasteful: the solid title band paints over the bottom 43% of the
       artwork, so nearly half of the picture is printed and then covered.

       So on paper the hero is a two-row grid, the image is an ordinary in-flow item in
       the first row, and the title band is the second. No absolute positioning, no
       overlap, and the art gets the whole of its own band.

       `min-height:0` on both, so the 1fr rows may shrink to what is left — a grid
       item's automatic minimum size is its content, and without it the details band
       gets pushed off the sheet. */
    .tk__hero{ height:auto; min-height:0; border-radius:0; overflow:hidden;
      display:grid; grid-template-rows:1fr auto; }
    .tk__shot{ position:static; grid-row:1; width:100%; height:100%; min-height:0;
      object-fit:cover;
      /* A framed image is already a 3:2 crop somebody chose — but this band is about
         124 × 36mm, i.e. 3.4:1, so a slice of that choice still has to be taken and
         somebody has to decide which. Centred loses the top of the picture, and the top
         of an event poster is where faces are; the screen band picks 32% for exactly the
         same reason. 38% rather than the screen's 32% because the slice here is much
         thinner, so the same bias would walk the subject straight off the top edge. */
      object-position:50% 38%;
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact }
    /* ── ON PAPER THE TYPE GETS A SOLID BAND, NOT A GRADIENT ────────────────
       The screen wash is calibrated against a 268px band. Printed, the artwork panel
       is about a third of that, so the same percentage stops put the 6.5pt date up
       where the wash has already cleared — gold on whatever the top of the poster
       happens to be, which on a light cover measures under 2:1.

       A gradient cannot be made safe for an unknown image at an unknown height, so
       print does not use one: the hero becomes art above and a solid accent band
       below, and `D.ink` is computed for exactly that accent. Same shape as the
       artboard, and legible by construction rather than by measurement. */
    .tk__scrim{ display:none }
    /* The second row of the hero grid, under the art rather than over it. `static` is
       safe now precisely because the image is no longer in the positioned layer — when
       it was, a static title painted underneath it and the ticket printed with no title
       on it at all. Both are in flow, so grid placement decides and nothing overlaps. */
    .tk__title{ position:static; grid-row:2; left:auto; right:auto; bottom:auto;
      padding:2.4mm 4mm 3mm; gap:1mm; background:var(--tk-accent);
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact; }
    .tk__kick{ font-size:7.5pt; line-height:1.15; letter-spacing:.22em }
    /* 1.12 and not the screen's 1.0: Yoruba orthography puts dots under o and e, and at
       a line-height of 1 the band's own edge cut them off — which turns Ọmọ into Omo on
       the one document that carries somebody's name. */
    .tk__title h1, .tk__title h1.is-long{ font-size:21pt; line-height:1.12 }
    .tk__sub{ font-size:7.5pt; line-height:1.15; letter-spacing:.14em }

    /* STUB RIGHT. 0.4mm dashed at 55% ink — a real perforation, and the one thing
       on the sheet that is heavier on paper than on screen, because the notches
       print as white circles on a white page, i.e. as nothing. */
    /* `overflow:hidden` is the safety valve. A four-line address is the one string here
       with no length limit upstream, and if something has to be lost off the bottom of a
       cut-to-size stub it must be the tail of the address — never the symbol, which is
       why the scan block is first in the DOM and therefore first on the sheet. */
    .tk__stub{ border-radius:0; border-left:.4mm dashed rgba(16,41,44,.55);
      padding:4mm; gap:2mm; align-items:center; justify-content:center;
      overflow:hidden;
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact; }
    /* The details band. `align-content:end` so a short field set sits against the foot
       of the artwork rather than floating in the middle of its own row. */
    .tk__facts{ border-radius:0; padding:2.6mm 4mm 3mm; gap:0; overflow:hidden;
      -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact; }

    .tk__scan{ gap:1.4mm; width:100% }
    /* 30mm, matching TicketPdf::QR_MM — the one measurement on this document that is a
       physical fact rather than a taste. 26mm was what the old 46mm stub could hold, not
       what a handheld reader wants off an inkjet. */
    .tk__qr{ width:30mm; padding:0 }
    .tk__qr svg{ width:30mm; height:30mm; border-radius:0; shape-rendering:crispEdges }
    .tk__codewrap{ gap:1mm }
    .tk__code{ font-size:11pt; line-height:1.05; letter-spacing:.18em; margin-right:-.18em }
    .tk__who{ display:block; font-size:9.5pt; line-height:1.1; font-weight:700;
      text-align:center; color:#000 }
    /* The screen caption is an instruction for a person holding a phone. On paper
       the code is simply there to be read. */
    .tk__qrnote{ display:none }

    /* ── WHAT FITS IN 46 × 62 MM, MEASURED ──────────────────────────────────
       A 26mm symbol is 42% of the sheet's height. That is the price of a code that
       scans off an inkjet, and it is not negotiable — below about 22mm a handheld
       reader starts failing on printed output.

       What is left is 2.5mm of padding at each end, the symbol, its code, and about
       97px at 96dpi. At the spec's ink-safe scale — 6pt labels, 8.5pt values — a
       single-line row is 23.7px and a wrapped one 36.4px, so the sheet holds FOUR:
       date and ticket type across two columns, then the address and the holder full
       width because those are the two that wrap. Everything else is marked
       `--nopaper` in the markup, with the reasoning beside it.

       Two columns stay two, not `auto-fit`: against a print context auto-fit resolves
       the available width differently across engines and can put six cells on one
       line at 3pt. */
    /* Three columns across 102mm. Fixed, not `auto-fit`: against a print context
       auto-fit resolves the available width differently across engines and can put six
       cells on one line at 3pt. At ~31mm a column takes `05 Sep 2026 · 5:00 pm` without
       wrapping, which is what stopped this fitting in the 46mm stub. */
    /* Three columns across 102mm, weighted rather than equal: the middle one carries
       `05 Sep 2026 · 5:00 pm`, which is half again as wide as `Standard` or a name, and
       at equal thirds it wrapped onto a second line and cost the artwork 12px of band.
       Fixed, not `auto-fit`: against a print context auto-fit resolves the available
       width differently across engines and can put six cells on one line at 3pt. */
    .tk__grid{ grid-template-columns:1.35fr 1fr; gap:1.8mm 4mm;
      padding-top:0; border-top:0 }
    .tk__cell{ gap:0 }
    .tk__cell--wide{ grid-column:1 / -1 }
    /* ── THE HOLDER IS PRINTED ONCE, NOT TWICE ─────────────────────────────
       It was in this band AND under the code on the stub, which is the same name
       twice on a card 62mm tall. The stub's copy is the one that has to stay: the
       stub is the half that tears off and is kept by the door, and a torn-off stub
       with a code and no name is not a record of who came in.

       Dropping it here is what pays for the rest. The band goes from three columns
       to two, so the address gets a full 102mm line instead of sharing it, the date
       stops wrapping, and the row that disappears is ~3mm of height that goes back
       to the artwork. */
    .tk__cell--holder{ display:none }
    .tk__cell small{ font-size:6.5pt; line-height:1.15; letter-spacing:.18em; color:#3d4749 }
    .tk__cell b{ font-size:10pt; line-height:1.14; color:#000 }
    .tk__cell--screen{ display:none }
    .tk__cell--print{ display:flex }
    /* The optional set stays on screen and comes off the sheet. The band is 102mm wide
       but only about 20mm tall, and these are the cells a door does not read. */
    .tk__cell--nopaper{ display:none }
    .tk__dot{ width:1.8mm; height:1.8mm; margin-right:1.2mm }

    .tk__note2{ display:none }
    /* Screen only. On 46mm the domain is already the head of the printed recovery URL,
       and a second mark is decoration the sheet cannot pay for. */
    .tk__sig{ display:none }
    /* In the details band rather than on the stub: the band is 102mm wide, so the URL
       sits on one line there instead of breaking across three in a 40mm column. */
    .tk__url{ display:block; margin-top:1.6mm; font-family:var(--ag-font-mono, monospace);
      font-size:6.5pt; line-height:1.25; color:#3d4749; word-break:break-all }

    /* A state that matters on paper is a state the door has to see. It goes under
       the stub's own fields, in words — never a colour alone. */
    .tk__flag{ display:none }
    .tk__flag--dead, .tk__flag--wait{ display:block; margin:1mm 0 0; padding:1mm 1.4mm;
      border-radius:0; border:.3mm solid #000; background:#fff; color:#000;
      font-size:6pt; line-height:1.25; }

    /* Everything that is not the ticket. `.ag-mobnav` is the one that caught this
       out once: the bottom bar printed across the foot of every ticket because the
       guess at its class name was never checked against a rendered page. Print CSS
       fails silently — there is no console warning for a selector that matches
       nothing — so this list is verified against the real markup. */
    .tk__acts, .tk__foot, .tk__meta, .tk__self, .ag-nav, .ag-announce, .ag-mobnav,
    .ag-foot, .afg-tabbar, .community-fab, .scroll-prog, .toast-host, .ag-loader,
    .skip-link, .gee-fab, .gee-panel, .gee-launch, .afg-drawer{ display:none !important }

    /* The site stylesheet expands every link to its href on paper. Inside a ticket
       that turns "the event page" into a line of URL through the middle of the card. */
    .tk a[href]::after{ content:none !important }
  }

  /* ── SELF-SERVICE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Collapsed by default and dropped from print. This page opens at a door; a form
     must never be between somebody and their QR code. */
  .tk__self{ width:100%; max-width:390px; margin:0 auto 28px; padding:0 20px;
    box-sizing:border-box; }
  @media (max-width:430px){ .tk__self{ max-width:none; padding:0 16px } }
  .tk__d{ border:1px solid #dde5e4; border-radius:12px; background:#fff }
  .tk__d summary{ cursor:pointer; padding:14px 16px; min-height:48px; display:flex;
    align-items:center; font-size:14.5px; font-weight:600; color:#10292C; border-radius:12px }
  .tk__d summary:focus-visible{ outline:3px solid #237b22; outline-offset:2px }
  .tk__dbody{ padding:2px 16px 18px; border-top:1px solid #eef3f2 }
  .tk__dnote{ font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.65; color:#5a6d6f; margin:12px 0 }
  .tk__act2{ margin:10px 0 }
  .tk__lab{ display:block; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; color:#3d5457; margin:12px 0 4px }
  /* 16px: anything smaller and iOS zooms the page on focus. */
  .tk__in{ width:100%; box-sizing:border-box; font-size:16px; padding:11px 12px; min-height:46px;
    border:1px solid #cfdad8; border-radius:9px; background:#fff; color:#10292C }
  .tk__in:focus{ outline:3px solid #237b22; outline-offset:1px; border-color:#237b22 }
  .tk__btn{ margin-top:12px; min-height:46px; padding:0 20px; font-size:14.5px; font-weight:650;
    border:0; border-radius:999px; background:#10292C; color:#fff; cursor:pointer }
  .tk__btn--ghost{ background:#fff; color:#10292C; border:1px solid #cfdad8; width:100%; margin-top:0 }
  .tk__btn:disabled{ opacity:.55; cursor:default }
  /* Destructive, and deliberately not red-by-default: it sits inside a panel somebody opened
     on purpose, and a page of alarm colours makes the one that matters invisible. Outlined
     until hover, and the WORD carries the meaning — never the colour alone. */
  .tk__btn--danger{ background:#fff; color:#a32b1f; border:1.5px solid #a32b1f }
  .tk__btn--danger:hover{ background:#a32b1f; color:#fff }
  .tk__fs{ border:0; border-top:1px solid #eef3f2; margin:18px 0 0; padding:14px 0 0 }
  .tk__fs legend{ font-size:13.5px; font-weight:700; color:#10292C; padding:0 }
  .tk__said{ margin:14px 0 0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6; min-height:1.2em }
  .tk__said.is-ok{ color:#1c5f1b } .tk__said.is-no{ color:#a32b1f }
  /* `@media screen`, not a bare rule. Bare, it sits AFTER the print block in this file and
     wins on source order at equal specificity — so the print caption was in the document,
     measured, and invisible on the one medium it exists for. */
  @media screen { .tk__cut{ display:none } }
  @media print { .tk__self{ display:none } }
</style>
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{#
  `where` is resolved once, at the top, because the address is used in the stub and again in
  the printed recovery block. Filtered rather than joined blind: an event with a venue and no
  city must not print a trailing comma.
#}
{% set where = event ? [event.venue|default(''), event.location|default('')]|filter(v => v) : [] %}

<div class="tk{{ dark ? ' tk--dark' : '' }}">

{% if reg is null %}
  <div class="tk__lost">
    <h1>This ticket link is not working</h1>
    <p>It may have been mistyped, or belong to a registration that was cancelled. Nothing has
      gone wrong with any payment you made.</p>
    <p>Write to <a href="mailto:{{ support_email|default('') }}">{{ support_email|default('the team') }}</a>
      with the name and email you registered with and a person will find it.</p>
  </div>

{% else %}
  {# ── PRICED IS NOT PAID ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `amount_naira > 0` says a price was set. It does not say the money arrived, and the
     two got conflated: a pending booking rendered `Admit one · paid` in the header and
     `Paid ₦75,000` in the grid, on a stub deliberately showing no code because the
     payment has NOT been seen. That is a document arguing with itself at a door, and the
     person holding it has every reason to believe the half that says paid.

     So the word `paid` is only used where the status confirms it, and a priced booking
     that is not yet confirmed reads `Price` — the same wording a free ticket gets, for
     the same reason: it is the amount, not a receipt. #}
  {% set priced = (reg.amount_naira|default(0)) > 0 %}
  {% set paid   = priced and reg.status == 'confirmed' %}
  {% set title  = event.title|default('Africa GATES event') %}

  {# ── the header row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  <header class="tk__meta">
    <span class="tk__admit">{{ paid ? 'Admit one · paid' : 'Admit one' }}</span>
    <span class="tk__ref">{{ reg.reference }}</span>
  </header>

  {# ══ THE DOCUMENT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <div class="tk__doc">

    {# ── the hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <section class="tk__hero">
      {% if D.image %}
        {# A real <img> rather than a background-image: it is content-adjacent, it is the
           thing that makes a ticket recognisably this event's, and a static `style` beside
           an Alpine `:style` is what AlpineStyleClobberTest fails the build over — so
           nothing here is bound.

           EAGER, and high priority. This is the largest above-the-fold paint on the page:
           lazy-loading defers the request until layout has run, which is the standard way
           to push LCP past 2.5s, and the event cover is served at poster resolution — so
           the deferral cost real seconds on a phone at a door.

           aria-hidden, because the title it sits behind says the same thing in words. #}
        <img class="tk__shot" src="{{ D.image }}" alt=""
             fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" aria-hidden="true">
      {% endif %}
      {# The wash. Four stops rather than two: opaque through the type block so the date and
         the title stay legible whatever the photograph does, and clear near the top so the
         artwork is still the artwork. Built from the validated accent, which is what keeps
         EventTicketDesign::contrastInk's promise true. The measurement behind each stop is
         in the stylesheet above. #}
      <span class="tk__scrim" aria-hidden="true"
            style="background:linear-gradient(to top,{{ D.accent }}F7 0%,{{ D.accent }}F2 48%,{{ D.accent }}B0 62%,{{ D.accent }}00 78%)"></span>
      <div class="tk__title">
        {# The kicker, in the compact form a ticket uses — `05.09.26 · IGANDO, LAGOS`. The
           written-out date is in the stub, where it is read rather than recognised. #}
        <p class="tk__kick">
          {%- if event.event_date|default('') %}{{ event.event_date|date('d.m.y') }}{% endif -%}
          {%- if event.event_date|default('') and event.location|default('') %} · {% endif -%}
          {{ event.location|default('')|upper }}
        </p>
        <h1{{ title|length > 26 ? ' class="is-long"' : '' }}>{{ title }}</h1>
        {% if event.tagline|default('') %}<p class="tk__sub">{{ event.tagline }}</p>{% endif %}
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="tk__tear" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></div>

    {# ── the stub ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <section class="tk__stub">

      {# QR and code, always both — never one or the other. A camera fails: a cracked
         screen, a dead battery lending somebody else's phone, low light, an organiser on a
         device whose browser has no barcode support. The printed code is the fallback, and
         it is the same string the QR contains, so the two can never disagree.

         {@see \AfricaGates\Support\Qr} returns null for anything it cannot encode, and this
         renders the code alone in that case rather than a broken image.

         Nothing here for a booking that is not confirmed. A pending payment rendered as a
         scannable ticket is an argument at a door, and the person having it is the attendee
         rather than us. #}
      {% if reg.status == 'confirmed' %}
        <div class="tk__scan">
          {% if qr %}<div class="tk__qr">{{ qr|raw }}</div>{% endif %}
          <div class="tk__codewrap">
            {# Selectable, and not an image: it is what somebody reads out when a scanner
               will not read their screen. #}
            <b class="tk__code">{{ reg.ticket_code|default('—') }}</b>
            {% if qr %}<span class="tk__qrnote">Scan it, or read the code out</span>{% endif %}
            {# Print only, and inside the scan block rather than the grid below: on the
               printed sheet this element IS the tear-off stub, and the name of the person
               it admits belongs on the half that gets torn off and kept. #}
            <span class="tk__who">{{ reg.name }}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      {% endif %}
    </section>

    {# ── the facts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <section class="tk__facts">

      {# ── the details ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Which optional cells appear is the organiser's choice — `D.rows` — because an
         organiser running a free community day does not want a "Paid ₦0" cell, and one
         running a gala very much wants the table number. Holder, date and address are not
         switchable: a ticket without them is not a ticket, and on paper they are all that
         survives the accent fill being dropped by a driver. #}
      <div class="tk__grid">
        {% if event.event_date|default('') %}
          {# Two cells on screen, one on paper. The screen has room for the day name —
             "Saturday" is what somebody checks against their own week — and 46mm of stub
             does not, so print gets the joined form below instead of both. #}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--screen"><small>Date</small>
            <b>{{ event.event_date|date('D d M Y') }}</b></div>
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--screen"><small>Doors</small>
            <b>{{ event.event_date|date('g:i a') }}{% if event.end_date|default('') %} – {{ event.end_date|date('g:i a') }}{% endif %}</b></div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if reg.tier|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell"><small>Ticket type</small><b>
            {%- if tier_swatch %}<span class="tk__dot" aria-hidden="true"
                 style="background:{{ tier_swatch.fill }};border-color:{{ tier_swatch.edge }}"></span>{% endif -%}
            {{ reg.tier }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        {# A SEAT PRINTS. It is the one optional cell a door team acts on, so it keeps its
           place on paper while the rest of the optional set does not. #}
        {% if 'seat' in D.rows and reg.seat_label|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell"><small>Seat</small><b>{{ reg.seat_label }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}

        {# ── WHAT PAPER HAS NO ROOM FOR ────────────────────────────────────
           A 46 × 62 mm stub holds a 26mm symbol, its code and about four rows. These
           five are the ones a door does not read: the price is settled, the phone and
           the email are ours to look up, the count and the booking date are records
           rather than credentials. Dropped from the sheet, kept on the screen. #}
        {% if 'price' in D.rows %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--nopaper"><small>{{ paid ? 'Paid' : 'Price' }}</small>
            <b>{{ priced ? '₦' ~ reg.amount_naira|number_format : 'Free' }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'seats' in D.rows and (reg.quantity|default(1)) > 1 %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--nopaper"><small>Seats</small><b>{{ reg.quantity }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'phone' in D.rows and reg.phone|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--nopaper"><small>Phone</small><b>{{ reg.phone }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'bought' in D.rows and reg.created_at|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--nopaper"><small>Booked</small>
            <b>{{ reg.created_at|slice(0, 10) }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'email' in D.rows and reg.email|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--wide tk__cell--nopaper"><small>Email</small>
            <b>{{ reg.email }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}

        {# Print only, and only because 46mm of stub cannot carry the day name as well as
           the time. Same fact as the two screen cells above, in one line. #}
        {% if event.event_date|default('') %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--print"><small>Date</small>
            <b>{{ event.event_date|date('d M Y') }} · {{ event.event_date|date('g:i a') }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}

        {# Last, and both full width, because these two are the ones that wrap. #}
        {% if where|length %}
          <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--wide"><small>Where</small><b>{{ where|join(', ') }}</b></div>
        {% endif %}
        <div class="tk__cell tk__cell--wide tk__cell--holder"><small>Ticket holder</small>
          <b>{{ reg.name }}</b></div>
      </div>

      {# Paper cannot be clicked, and this is how somebody who printed a ticket and then
         lost the email finds it again. A bare line rather than a labelled cell: on a sheet
         with three rows to spare, a `REFERENCE` label above a URL that already ends in the
         reference is a row spent saying the same thing twice. #}
      <span class="tk__url">{{ site_url }}/events/ticket/{{ reg.reference|url_encode }}</span>

      {% if D.note %}<p class="tk__note2"><b>{{ D.note }}</b></p>{% endif %}

      {# The signature. Screen only: on 46mm of paper the domain is already in the printed
         recovery URL above, and a second mark is decoration the sheet has no room for. #}
      <div class="tk__sig">
        <img src="/assets/img/afg-mark.svg" alt="Africa GATES" width="26" height="26">
        <span>AGATES.ORG</span>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  {# ── the states ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% if reg.status == 'pending' %}
    <div class="tk__flag tk__flag--wait">
      <strong>We have not seen your payment yet.</strong> If your bank has debited you it
      can take a few minutes to reach us — this page will show your ticket code as soon as
      it does, so keep the link. Nothing further is needed from you.
    </div>
  {% elseif reg.status == 'cancelled' %}
    <div class="tk__flag tk__flag--dead">
      <strong>This registration was cancelled.</strong> If that is a surprise, it is usually
      a checkout that was never completed — nothing was charged. You are welcome to book again.
    </div>
  {% elseif reg.status == 'waitlisted' %}
    <div class="tk__flag tk__flag--wait">
      <strong>You are on the waiting list.</strong> This tier is full. If a seat comes free
      the longest-waiting person is offered it first, and you will be emailed — keep this
      link, because the offer appears here too.
    </div>
  {% elseif reg.status != 'confirmed' %}
    <div class="tk__flag tk__flag--wait">This registration is
      <strong>{{ reg.status|default('incomplete') }}</strong>.</div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if reg.checked_in_at|default('') %}
    {# The ticket stays visible. Afterwards it is a receipt. #}
    <div class="tk__flag tk__flag--in">Checked in at {{ reg.checked_in_at }}. Welcome.</div>
  {% endif %}

  {# ── the actions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Outside the document, so print drops them without leaving a gap in the ticket.

     The calendar link is a real download rather than a script: a paid seat that goes unused
     is usually somebody who meant to write the date down and did not.

     The second action is a GENERATED PDF and not the browser's print dialogue. `@media
     print` hands the page size, the margins and — the one that breaks things — the scale
     factor to the browser and the driver, and "Fit to page" is on by default in more than
     one of them: it rescales the QR below the module size a scanner can resolve, which
     nobody discovers until a gate with a queue behind it. The PDF is fixed millimetres. #}
  {% if event.event_date|default('') or reg.status == 'confirmed' %}
  <div class="tk__acts">
    {% if event.event_date|default('') %}
      <a class="tk__act tk__act--go" href="/events/ticket/{{ reg.reference|url_encode }}/calendar.ics">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="M8 3v4M16 3v4M3 11h18"/>
        </svg>Add to calendar</a>
    {% endif %}
    {% if reg.status == 'confirmed' %}
      <a class="tk__act tk__act--ghost" href="/events/ticket/{{ reg.reference|url_encode }}/ticket.pdf">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M12 3v12m0 0-4-4m4 4 4-4M4 17v2a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2v-2"/>
        </svg>Save or print</a>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# Print only — `.tk__cut` has no screen rule at all, and `.tk__foot` beside it is the
     screen's version of the same sentence and is hidden on paper. Two audiences, two
     sentences, neither of them shown to the other. #}
  <p class="tk__cut">Cut along the dotted line. At the door the stub tears off down the
    dashes — or just show the code on your phone.</p>

  <p class="tk__foot">Keep this link — it is your ticket, and it works without signing in to
    anything.{% if event.slug|default('') %} Full details are on the
    <a href="/events/{{ event.slug }}">event page</a>.{% endif %}</p>
  <p class="tk__foot"><b>Nothing about attending costs more than the price above.</b>
    If anybody asks you for a further payment to attend, it is not us.</p>
{% endif %}

</div>

{# ── SELF-SERVICE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The three things that otherwise arrive in a support inbox as free text and get done by
   hand. Collapsed behind <details> rather than laid out, and OUTSIDE the ticket frame: this
   page's job is to open at a door, and a form is the last thing somebody wants between them
   and their QR code.

   Not a cancel button up front. Giving a seat up is a money decision — the organiser may
   hold a deposit, the tier may be non-refundable — and a self-service cancel that silently
   did neither would be the worst of both. That one stays a conversation. #}
{% if reg is not null and reg.status == 'confirmed' and not reg.checked_in_at|default('') %}
<div class="tk__self">
  <details class="tk__d">
    <summary>Manage this ticket</summary>
    <div class="tk__dbody">

      <p class="tk__dnote">Changes are confirmed with a code sent to the email this ticket
        was booked with, so somebody who has only seen your QR code cannot alter it.</p>

      <div class="tk__act2">
        <button type="button" class="tk__btn tk__btn--ghost" data-tk="resend">Email me this ticket again</button>
      </div>

      <div class="tk__act2">
        <button type="button" class="tk__btn tk__btn--ghost" data-tk="code">Send me a code to make a change</button>
      </div>

      <div id="tkChange" hidden>
        <label class="tk__lab" for="tkCode">6-digit code</label>
        <input class="tk__in" id="tkCode" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="one-time-code"
               maxlength="6" placeholder="123456">

        <fieldset class="tk__fs">
          <legend>Change the name on the ticket</legend>
          <label class="tk__lab" for="tkName">Name</label>
          <input class="tk__in" id="tkName" maxlength="160" value="{{ reg.name }}">
          <button type="button" class="tk__btn" data-tk="rename">Save the new name</button>
        </fieldset>

        {# ── CANCELLING ────────────────────────────────────────────────
           The figure is fetched and shown BEFORE the confirm. A cancellation flow that
           reveals what you get back only after the irreversible step is the pattern
           regulators enforce against, and it is also how somebody ends up furious with an
           organiser who did nothing wrong. The button is deliberately quiet and last: it is
           the one action here nobody should hit by accident. #}
        <fieldset class="tk__fs">
          <legend>Cancel this booking</legend>
          <p class="tk__dnote" id="tkCancelWhy">Checking what the organiser's policy gives
            back…</p>
          <button type="button" class="tk__btn tk__btn--danger" data-tk="cancel" hidden
                  id="tkCancelGo">Cancel and give up my place</button>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="tk__fs">
          <legend>Give this ticket to somebody else</legend>
          <label class="tk__lab" for="tkTName">Their name</label>
          <input class="tk__in" id="tkTName" maxlength="160" placeholder="Who is coming instead">
          <label class="tk__lab" for="tkTMail">Their email</label>
          <input class="tk__in" id="tkTMail" type="email" placeholder="them@example.com">
          <p class="tk__dnote">We email them the ticket with a new code, and
            <strong>this one stops working at the door</strong>.</p>
          <button type="button" class="tk__btn" data-tk="transfer">Transfer the ticket</button>
        </fieldset>
      </div>

      {# One live region for every reply, so a screen reader hears the outcome wherever it
         came from, and sighted users are not hunting for which form answered. #}
      <p class="tk__said" id="tkSaid" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
  </details>
</div>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block foot_scripts %}
{% if reg is not null %}
{# The only script on the page, and nothing depends on it: `data-tk` attributes rather than
   inline handlers, because CspTest fails the build on an inline event handler. #}
<script nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
/* ── SELF-SERVICE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One handler for four endpoints, because the reply shape is identical and the only thing
   that differs is which fields go up. Every outcome lands in ONE live region: a screen
   reader hears it wherever it came from, and nobody has to work out which form answered. */
(function () {
  var wrap = document.getElementById('tkSaid');
  if (!wrap) return;
  var base = {{ ('/events/ticket/' ~ reg.reference ~ '/')|json_encode|raw }};
  var change = document.getElementById('tkChange');

  function say(ok, msg) {
    wrap.className = 'tk__said ' + (ok ? 'is-ok' : 'is-no');
    wrap.textContent = msg || '';
  }

  async function post(path, payload, btn) {
    var label = btn ? btn.textContent : '';
    if (btn) { btn.disabled = true; btn.textContent = 'Working…'; }
    say(true, '');
    try {
      var r = await fetch(base + path, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest',
                   /* These endpoints CHANGE the ticket, so they go through CSRF like every
                      other write on the platform. The door is the exception, not this. */
                   'X-CSRF-Token': {{ csrf_token|json_encode|raw }} },
        body: JSON.stringify(payload || {})
      });
      var j = await r.json();
      say(!!j.success, j.message || '');
      return j;
    } catch (e) {
      say(false, 'That did not go through. Check your connection and try again.');
      return null;
    } finally {
      if (btn) { btn.disabled = false; btn.textContent = label; }
    }
  }

  /* The quote is fetched when the panel OPENS, so the refund figure is already on screen by
     the time somebody reaches the cancel button — never after they have pressed it. */
  var det = document.querySelector('.tk__d');
  var quoted = false;
  if (det) {
    det.addEventListener('toggle', async function () {
      if (!det.open || quoted) return;
      quoted = true;
      var why = document.getElementById('tkCancelWhy');
      var go  = document.getElementById('tkCancelGo');
      try {
        var r = await fetch(base + 'cancel-quote', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest',
                     'X-CSRF-Token': {{ csrf_token|json_encode|raw }} },
          body: '{}'
        });
        var q = await r.json();
        if (why) {
          why.textContent = q.message || '';
          if (q.policy_text) { why.textContent += ' ' + q.policy_text; }
          if (!q.success && q.contact) {
            why.textContent += ' To cancel, contact ' + q.contact + '.';
          }
        }
        if (go && q.success) { go.hidden = false; go.setAttribute('data-owed', String(q.naira || 0)); }
      } catch (e) {
        if (why) { why.textContent = 'We could not check the refund policy just now.'; }
      }
    });
  }

  document.addEventListener('click', async function (e) {
    var btn = e.target.closest ? e.target.closest('[data-tk]') : null;
    if (!btn) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    var what = btn.getAttribute('data-tk');
    var code = (document.getElementById('tkCode') || {}).value || '';

    if (what === 'resend')   { await post('resend', {}, btn); return; }
    if (what === 'code') {
      var j = await post('code', {}, btn);
      /* The change fields appear only once a code is actually on its way. Showing them up
         front would invite somebody to fill in a transfer and then be told to go and fetch
         a code — which is the point at which they email support instead. */
      if (j && j.success && change) { change.hidden = false;
        var f = document.getElementById('tkCode'); if (f) f.focus(); }
      return;
    }
    if (what === 'rename') {
      await post('rename', { code: code, name: (document.getElementById('tkName') || {}).value || '' }, btn);
      return;
    }
    if (what === 'cancel') {
      var why = document.getElementById('tkCancelWhy');
      var owed = btn.getAttribute('data-owed') || '0';
      /* The amount is repeated in the confirm, not just above it. This is the last moment
         before something irreversible, and it is the moment somebody actually reads. */
      var line = owed === '0'
        ? 'Give up your place? Nothing is refunded, and your ticket code stops working.'
        : 'Give up your place and refund ₦' + Number(owed).toLocaleString()
          + '? Your ticket code stops working.';
      if (!window.confirm(line)) return;
      var c = await post('cancel', { code: code }, btn);
      if (c && c.success) {
        if (why) { why.textContent = ''; }
        btn.hidden = true;
        setTimeout(function () { location.reload(); }, 3000);
      }
      return;
    }
    if (what === 'transfer') {
      var nm = (document.getElementById('tkTName') || {}).value || '';
      var em = (document.getElementById('tkTMail') || {}).value || '';
      /* Confirmed in the browser as well as refused on the server, because this is the one
         action on the page that cannot be undone by the person taking it: after it, their
         own code stops working. */
      if (!window.confirm('Transfer this ticket to ' + (nm || 'them') + '? Your own code will stop working at the door.')) return;
      var t = await post('transfer', { code: code, name: nm, email: em }, btn);
      if (t && t.success) { setTimeout(function () { location.reload(); }, 2500); }
      return;
    }
  });
})();
</script>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
